  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "8lz8QJnKJXpNOAvgS5SNqj4ZIb0=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:54:09.566Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The Academy states the fork explicitly at the top of the Build chapter: this is the **manual experience** \u2014 building packages by hand with the Powerhouse CLI, controlling document models, editors, processors and publishing \u2014 while the **AI-guided experience** lives in Vetra Studio, where you describe what you want in chat and the agent writes the specs and code, with **no local setup required**.\n\nThe important part is the sentence that follows: \"the two paths produce the same kind of package, so you can mix and match \u2014 prototype with the agent, then drop down to the manual workflow for fine-grained control.\" That makes the choice reversible rather than architectural.\n\nFor the manual path the machine needs three things \u2014 **Node.js 24** to run the code, **VS Code** to write it, **Git** to manage it \u2014 plus the CLI: `pnpm install -g ph-cmd` (or npm), with a global install recommended so the command works from any directory. If those three are already present, installing `ph-cmd` is the only step.\n\n**Windows users are steered to WSL** rather than native Windows: `wsl --install` in an Administrator PowerShell, restart, create a Linux user, then follow the Linux (Ubuntu/Debian) instructions throughout. The stated reason is a development experience consistent with production environments and with most tutorials; VS Code still runs on Windows through the Remote-WSL extension.\n\n## Provenance\n- `apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/00-GettingStartedBuilding/01-Prerequisites.md` \u2014 tip 'Two ways to build: manual vs. AI-guided'; Overview; Windows Users: Consider Using WSL\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:54:09.564Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:54:09.566Z"
    }
  },
